<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phùng Hưng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 791</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Bố Cái Đại Vương</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phùng Hưng was a local leader in Tang-ruled Annan, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, who led a revolt against heavy taxation in the late 8th century. His forces took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>'s seat at Tống Bình, and he ruled briefly until his death, after which Tang authority was restored. He was later honoured as Bố Cái Đại Vương, the 'great king, father and mother of the people'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phung-hung.jpg" alt="Phùng Hưng" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Doãn Hiệu at Vietnamese Wikipedia · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TuongPhungHung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
